<%# §8'S BOTTOM HALF: suggestions, always running, derived from entry history. Local: `presenter`.

    THIS SECTION IS WHY THE PAGE IS PERMANENT RATHER THAN A WIZARD. Two of the four kinds — drift
    and a dead rule — are about rules that already exist and have quietly stopped matching what the
    user actually spends, and a one-time setup can never catch either.

    THERE IS A DISMISS CONTROL, AND THERE DELIBERATELY WAS NOT (Henry's ruling of 2026-08-20,
    from real use). The argument that stood here is worth keeping because it was not wrong about
    anything it named: dismissal is STATE, the state it would hide is DRIFT, and the usual escape —
    resurfacing after N periods — is nagging with extra machinery. §8 concluded from that that a
    suggestion should leave this list only by being ACTED ON.

    WHAT REAL USE ANSWERED IS THE STEP THAT ARGUMENT SKIPPED. A panel with no way to set one row
    aside does not get read row by row; it gets ignored WHOLE — which hides all four kinds at once,
    drift included, and does it silently. Hiding one is the smaller loss, and the ruling makes it
    the smaller loss on purpose:

      · IT IS THE USER'S ACT, one row at a time, never the page's. Nothing here decides to drop a
        suggestion, and nothing expires.
      · IT IS NOT A DELETION. Every hidden row is listed at this panel's foot with a "Show" button,
        so "where did it go" always has an answer and the count is always on screen.
      · IT HIDES A PAIR, NOT A SENTENCE. `suggestion_dismissals` stores (kind, subject); the
        suggestion is still derived on every load, so a hidden drift whose figures worsen is the
        same hidden row saying the worse figure the moment it is shown again.

    EVERY suggestion the engine returns that the user has not hidden is rendered, in the engine's
    own order (per-period cost, not sticker size) — nothing is truncated here, because truncation
    would be a dismissal with no button and no foot section to find it in. %>
<section class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded" data-suggestions>
  <div class="px-6 py-4 border-b border-gray-200">
    <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900">What your spending suggests</h3>
    <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-gray-500">
      Read from your entries every time this page loads. Nothing here changes anything until you accept it.
    </p>
  </div>

  <% if presenter.suggestions.empty? %>
    <%# THE EMPTY STATE, and it is a real answer rather than a gap: the detectors ran and found
        nothing. A brand-new user with no entries at all lands here too, and so does a user whose
        every bill already has a rule — which is the state this panel exists to get them to.

        A USER WHO HAS HIDDEN EVERY SUGGESTION READS THIS TOO, and the foot section below is what
        keeps that honest: the sentence says nothing is left to suggest, and directly under it sits
        "N hidden suggestions" saying where the rest went. %>
    <div class="px-6 py-8 text-center">
      <p class="text-sm text-gray-500">
        Nothing to suggest — every bill and rate we can see in your entries already has a rule.
      </p>
    </div>
  <% else %>
    <%# THE INDEX (Task 7's review). On the demo this panel is about 5,000px tall, and truncation
        is still forbidden — a panel that hid the cheapest half would be a dismissal with no button
        and nothing at the foot to find it in, which is precisely what the Hide control is NOT.
        What this strip does instead is NAVIGATION — say how many of each kind there are and let
        the reader jump — which hides nothing at all. Every suggestion below is still rendered, in
        the engine's own order.

        The counts and the headings come from ONE grouping (`suggestions_by_kind`), so a count in
        this strip cannot disagree with the run of rows it points at. The engine sorts by kind
        first, so each run is contiguous by construction and this adds no ordering of its own. %>
    <nav class="px-6 py-3 border-b border-gray-200 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-3 gap-y-1 text-sm"
         aria-label="Suggestions by kind"
         data-suggestions-index>
      <% presenter.suggestions_by_kind.each_with_index do |(kind, group), index| %>
        <span class="text-gray-300" aria-hidden="true"><%= "·" unless index.zero? %></span>
        <%= link_to suggestion_kind_count(kind, group.size),
                    "##{suggestion_kind_anchor(kind)}",
                    class: "text-brand-dark hover:text-brand underline",
                    data: { suggestions_index_link: kind } %>
      <% end %>
    </nav>

    <% presenter.suggestions_by_kind.each do |kind, group| %>
      <%# The anchor target and the run's own heading are one element: a bare `<span id>` would be
          a landing spot with nothing at it, and a reader who jumped would have to infer from the
          rows what they had jumped to. `scroll-mt-4` keeps the heading off the very top edge of
          the viewport after the jump. %>
      <div id="<%= suggestion_kind_anchor(kind) %>"
           class="px-6 py-2 bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100 scroll-mt-4"
           data-suggestions-group="<%= kind %>">
        <%# Sentence case, not the `uppercase` treatment this codebase gives short labels like
            "Current Balance": "RULES THAT HAVE DRIFTED" shouts a phrase, which is the objection
            home/_attention.html.erb already records about its own band heading. It also made the
            rendered text differ from the markup, so an assertion about the words failed against a
            page that was showing them. %>
        <h4 class="text-xs font-semibold tracking-wide text-gray-500">
          <%= suggestion_kind_heading(kind) %> · <%= group.size %>
        </h4>
      </div>

      <% group.each do |suggestion| %>
        <%= render "budget_page/suggestion", suggestion: suggestion %>
      <% end %>
    <% end %>
  <% end %>

  <%# WHERE THE HIDDEN ONES WENT (Henry's ruling of 2026-08-20). OUTSIDE the empty-state branch,
      deliberately: a user who has hidden every suggestion reads "Nothing to suggest" above, and
      this is the line that stops that sentence from being a lie.

      COLLAPSED, NOT ABSENT. `<details>` rather than a Stimulus controller — the whole interaction
      is "open this" and the browser already has it, so there is no JavaScript here to be broken by
      a Turbo navigation. The COUNT is in the summary, always visible, because the count is the
      part that has to be true whether or not anyone opens it.

      NO INDEX AND NO KIND HEADINGS. This is a list to find one row in and put it back, not a
      second panel: the rows carry the kind's own sentence and a Show button, and the accept-effect
      clause above would be describing an act nobody can perform from here. %>
  <% if presenter.hidden_suggestions.any? %>
    <details class="border-t border-gray-200" data-hidden-suggestions>
      <summary class="px-6 py-3 text-sm text-gray-500 cursor-pointer hover:text-gray-700">
        <%= pluralize(presenter.hidden_suggestions.size, "hidden suggestion") %>
      </summary>

      <% presenter.hidden_suggestions.each do |hidden| %>
        <article class="px-6 py-4 border-t border-gray-100 flex flex-col sm:flex-row sm:items-start sm:justify-between gap-3"
                 data-hidden-suggestion="<%= suggestion_key(hidden.suggestion) %>">
          <div class="text-sm text-gray-500 space-y-1">
            <%= render "budget_page/suggestion_#{hidden.suggestion.kind}", suggestion: hidden.suggestion %>
          </div>

          <div class="shrink-0">
            <%# THE ROW'S OWN ID, not the pair the Hide button carried: this is a record now, and
                `destroy` finds it through `current_user.suggestion_dismissals`. %>
            <%= button_to "Show",
                          suggestion_dismissal_path(hidden.dismissal),
                          method: :delete,
                          class: "btn btn-secondary whitespace-nowrap" %>
          </div>
        </article>
      <% end %>
    </details>
  <% end %>
</section>
